.TH DREM 3 "June 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
drem, dremf \- floating-point remainder (obsolescent)
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double drem(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float dremf(float " x ", float " y );
.PP
Feature-test macro requirements (see
.BR feature_test_macros (7)):
.PP
.RS 4
.BR drem (),
.BR dremf ():
.RS 4
_DEFAULT_SOURCE || _XOPEN_SOURCE || _SVID_SOURCE || _BSD_SOURCE
.RE
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR drem ()
function is an obsolescent alias for
.BR remainder (3).
It computes the IEEE 754 remainder of dividing
.I x
by
.IR y :
the value
.IR "x \- n*y" ,
where
.I n
is the integer nearest the exact value of
.IR x / y .
If there are two integers equally near, then
.I n
is chosen to be even.  The result may have either sign and its
magnitude is at most
.IR "|y|/2" .
.PP
.BR drem ()
and
.BR dremf ()
are thin forwarders: they call
.BR remainder (3)
and
.BR remainderf (3)
respectively and return their results unchanged.  They carry no
behaviour of their own.
.PP
.B WARNING:
.BR drem ()
is obsolescent.  New code should call
.BR remainder (3)
directly.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The return value, errors, and special-case behaviour are exactly those
of
.BR remainder (3).
On success, the floating-point remainder of
.IR "x / y" ,
as described above, is returned.
.SH ERRORS
The error behaviour is that of
.BR remainder (3).
.PP
The following error can occur:
.TP
Domain error:
.I x
is an infinity or
.I y
is 0, and the other argument is not a NaN
A NaN is returned and an invalid floating-point exception
.RB ( FE_INVALID )
is raised.  If
.I "math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO"
is non-zero,
.I errno
is set to
.BR EDOM .
.PP
See
.BR math_error (7)
for information on how to determine whether an error has occurred when
calling these functions.
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR drem (),
.BR dremf ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
None.  Obsolescent.
.SH HISTORY
4.3BSD.  Renamed
.BR remainder ()
in C99 and POSIX.1-2001;
.BR drem ()
is retained as a legacy alias.
